'''Guaji''' is the principal language of the [[Guaji people]] of [[Guajicutea]]. It is spoken by roughly 1.4 million people as a first language and by many more as a second language, especially in mixed urban households. The language developed from the island's Taino/Arawak-inspired base, but over nearly 2,000 years on Adonia it acquired a tighter vowel system, stronger consonant lenition, and a dense set of island-specific terms for volcanic terrain, navigation, storms, trade, and sacred carving.

In modern Guajicutea, Guaji remains strongest in rural districts, fishing communities, and older coastal and inland settlements. [[Sartan language|Sartan]] is dominant in much of the state, but Guaji still carries cultural prestige in domestic life, local religion, music, and historical writing.

== History ==

=== Early divergence ===

The language probably diverged into recognisable western and eastern varieties during the long pre-516 CE separation between the [[Yukurí]] mountain communities and the [[Caracurí]] sea communities. The western variety preserved more conservative highland vocabulary and older ritual speech, while the eastern variety changed faster through trade and contact with other island communities. After the unification of Guajicutea in 516 CE, the eastern variety became the basis of later standard speech, but Yukurí forms survived in technical vocabulary, sacred terms, and highland place names.

The writing system associated with the language, [[Bikumaku]], is older than the formal kingdom. The earliest carved tablets and ritual records appear around the fourth century CE, before the unification of the island. Those early texts were pictographic and strongly tied to elite ritual practice. Later Bikumaku became more abstract, adding phonetic modifier strokes and a more regular system for recording names, dates, and ceremonial formulas.

=== Unification and sacred record ===

After the [[yukubara]] event and the burial of [[Guajicutea Vieja]] in 516 CE, Guaji became the common language of the unified island society. The catacombs, calendar corridor, and storm ledger established the language as the medium of sacred memory. Later generations continued to use Guaji for ritual speech, historical recitation, and carved records, even as everyday life shifted around it.

The language also expanded its vocabulary during the centuries of trade and state formation that followed. Guaji developed specialised words for mining, shipbuilding, weather, timekeeping, and catacomb architecture, reflecting the physical world of the island rather than any outside model.

=== Colonial and republican periods ===

Colonial contact brought new pressures. Paper entered Guajicutea through Burawi trade in the 1530s, and Guaji oral history began to be transcribed into the [[Mesogean script]] for secular use. That change did not replace Bikumaku, which remained the sacred carving tradition, but it did give the language a wider written life outside priestly circles. During the colonial and early republican periods, urban speech shifted heavily toward Sartan, while Guaji survived best in rural households, fishing villages, and older inland communities.

The 1947 constitution restored language protection and made both Guaji and Sartan common languages of public life. Since then, Guaji has remained an important national language, though one with a stronger rural and cultural base than Sartan.

== Phonology ==

Guaji has a compact vowel system of three phonemes: /a i u/. Earlier /e/ merged with /i/ and /o/ merged with /u/, leaving the language with a tighter, more closed sound than its earlier ancestor. The result is a language with frequent high vowels and a strong preference for short, clear syllables.

Common consonant changes include voicing of initial stops, lenition between vowels, and palatalisation before front vowels. In broad terms, the language favours /b g k m n r t y w/ with fewer fricatives than Sartan. A glottal stop is also common, especially where vowel sequences meet.

Typical syllable structure remains mostly simple, with a strong preference for open syllables. Final vowels are common, and many words end in a vowel followed by a light glottal catch.

