== Grammar ==

Guaji is mildly agglutinative, with productive suffixes and frequent compounding. Many of its most important words are built from a small set of roots combined with semantic suffixes that indicate place, movement, size, or collective identity. Head-final compounding is normal, so the modifying element usually comes first.

A few productive suffixes are especially common:

{| class="wikitable"
! Suffix !! Meaning !! Example
|-
| ''-í'' || people of / place of || ''Yukurí'', ''Caracurí'', ''Guanarí''
|-
| ''-á'' || augmentative / great feature || ''Yukumá''
|-
| ''-uma'' || extraction place / digging place || ''Yaruma''
|-
| ''-bara'' || flow / movement of || ''yukubara''
|}

This structure makes the language especially good at naming terrain. A mountain that flows is a mudflow, a mountain that bursts is an eruption, and a marked place is a carved or claimed space. That same logic runs through ordinary vocabulary, ritual naming, and place names.

== Dialects ==

=== Yukurí ===

Yukurí is the older western highland variety. It preserves more conservative forms, especially in mountain, cave, and volcanic vocabulary. It is also the variety most strongly associated with ritual speech, mining communities, and older ceremonial terminology.

=== Caracurí ===

Caracurí is the eastern coastal variety and the basis of the later standard language. It is more innovative, especially in trade, navigation, and household speech. After unification, Caracurí became the more widely used public variety, but it absorbed many Yukurí roots in technical domains.

=== Modern standard ===

Modern educated Guaji is a mixed standard. Its grammar and core vocabulary are mostly Caracurí-derived, while its mountain, storm, and catacomb terms often preserve Yukurí forms. Rural speech is generally more conservative than urban speech, and older speakers tend to preserve more local forms than younger bilingual speakers in Sartan-dominant cities.

== Writing system ==

The traditional writing system of Guaji is [[Bikumaku]], a logographic and later ideographic script used by priestly specialists. The earliest forms were pictographic and closely tied to the catacombs, the calendar corridor, and the storm ledger. In later centuries, Bikumaku gained more abstract forms and phonetic modifier strokes. A simplified description of the modifiers is often given as follows: horizontal strokes for /a/, vertical strokes for /i/, curved strokes for /u/, dots for /b/, crosses for /k/, and waves for /w/.

Bikumaku was never a mass-literacy script. It belonged to the religious and archival class, especially the [[bohíque]]s and priestesses who maintained the calendar and recorded major events. Its prestige remained high even after paper transcription became common in the colonial period.

Today, Guaji is usually written in the [[Mesogean script]] for schoolbooks, newspapers, government use, and general publishing. Bikumaku survives in ceremonial carving, historical study, and artistic revival, especially after the 19th-century rediscovery of the catacombs.

== Vocabulary ==

Guaji vocabulary reflects the island's material world. It has especially rich sets of terms for volcanic events, mining, storms, sea travel, and sacred architecture.

Examples include:

* ''yukubara'' — volcanic mudflow
* ''yukubúka'' — eruption
* ''wariku'' — to pan or wash sediment
* ''warí'' — moon / month
* ''wirá'' — hurricane
* ''bikú'' — carved year-mark
* ''bikumá'' — great ring or decadal mark
* ''kurubá'' — burial niche

These terms are not simply decorative vocabulary. They reflect the island's geology, political history, and religious practice. In Guaji, language and landscape have always been closely linked.

== Current status ==

Guaji remains a major national language, but it is not evenly distributed. It is strongest in rural households, fishing communities, older inland districts, and many working-class neighbourhoods outside the largest cities. Sartan dominates more fully in state administration, inter-island trade, and much of urban elite life, so most younger Guajians are bilingual.

The language has also gained renewed prestige through historical and cultural revival. The rediscovery of the catacombs in 1892, the historical [[Bikuismo movement]], and the modern Bikuismo revival led by [[Lidia Bikarí]] and formalised in the [[Bikumaku Language Act]] of 2021 all strengthened its public standing. Guaji now appears in literature, local radio, school instruction, ceremonial speech, family life, and, through Bikumaku, secondary government signage since 2021, even where it no longer holds the same dominance it once did.

